political tensions between the u.s and  china now playing out in next year's  winter olympics in beijing  u.s president joe biden said for weeks  he was considering a diplomatic boycott  of the games over china's human rights  record particularly what washington says  is genocide against uyghur muslims in  xingyang province the biden  administration will not send any  diplomatic or official representation to  the beijing 2022 winter olympics and  paralympic games given the pierre scenes  ongoing genocide and crimes against  humanity and xinjiang and other human  rights abuses the athletes on team usa  have our full support we will be behind  them 100 percent as we cheer them on  from home  china is accused of detaining more than  a million uyghur muslims and other  minorities in camps where forced labor  and sterilization of women are allegedly  common  beijing denies this before the boycott  was announced it warned of counter  measures but also talked down any likely  impact from the u.s move  those politicians who clamor for a  boycott for political self-interest are  just showing off and hyping things up  nobody cares whether they come or not  and it will have no influence on  beijing's success in hosting the winter  olympics  the boycott is moving forward despite  recent efforts to stabilize ties  including last month's video meeting  between biden and chinese president xi  jinping i think what we're seeing now is  both the united states and china testing  the waters and going back and forth on  politics and the olympics become a  platform or a proxy for engaging in  political verbal battles let's just hope  it stays at verbal we're entering a  perilous moment and the olympics are  sort of amping up the tension between  the two countries in ways that are much  bigger than the olympics  the u.s also applauded the women's  tennis association for suspending all  tournaments in china over concerns for  the safety of chinese tennis star  pengshui  with the olympics coming to the u.s in  2028 there are now questions about how  china may respond both in the short and  long term  katya lopezudayan al jazeera
 

